  15. Common Pitfalls & Troubleshooting

  Problem: OOM crash
  Cause: Manual cache tuning or too many active series
  Solution: Remove manual cache flags; add RAM
  ────────────────────────────────────────
  Problem: Slow queries
  Cause: Overly broad selectors like {__name__=~".*"}
  Solution: Use specific label matchers
  ────────────────────────────────────────
  Problem: Slow inserts > 5%
  Cause: TSID cache too small for active series count
  Solution: Add more RAM (don't tune caches)
  ────────────────────────────────────────
  Problem: Disk full
  Cause: Didn't account for retention + 1 month overhead
  Solution: retention uses retentionPeriod + ~1 month of disk
  ────────────────────────────────────────
  Problem: High cardinality
  Cause: Labels with frequently changing values
  Solution: Remove request IDs, timestamps from labels
  ────────────────────────────────────────
  Problem: Data gaps
  Cause: vmagent buffer overflow, network issues
  Solution: Check -remoteWrite.maxDiskUsagePerURL
  ────────────────────────────────────────
  Problem: Wrong query results
  Cause: Stale NaN handling differs from Prometheus
  Solution: MetricsQL removes NaN; use keep_last_value()
  ────────────────────────────────────────
  Problem: High memory after restart
  Cause: Index rebuild on startup
  Solution: Normal; caches warm up over time

  Production Best Practices

  1. In Kubernetes, set requests == limits for vmstorage (prevents OOM kills)
  2. Use default flags - VM auto-tunes for available resources
  3. Avoid labels with >30 unique values per label name
  4. Set -search.maxUniqueTimeseries to prevent runaway queries
  5. Use a separate monitoring instance to monitor your primary VM

